# the argument of epictetus-1.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# S2 is a rhetorical question, read as asserting its answer: wishing is not enough, one must learn.
# "must"/"necessity" in S2, S5 and S6 is read as practical obligation (ought), not metaphysical necessity.
logic	kd
atom	carpenter_by_learning	the carpenter becomes a carpenter when he has learned certain things
atom	pilot_by_learning	the pilot becomes a pilot by learning certain things
atom	wish_sufficient	in philosophy it is sufficient to wish to be wise and good
atom	learn_things	one learns certain things in philosophy
atom	learn_god_exists	one learns that there is a God
atom	learn_providence	one learns that God provides for all things
atom	learn_no_concealment	one learns that it is not possible to conceal from God our acts, intentions and thoughts
atom	would_please_gods	one would please and obey the gods
atom	like_gods	one tries with all his power to be like the gods
atom	divine_faithful	the divine is faithful
atom	man_faithful	man is faithful
atom	divine_free	the divine is free
atom	man_free	man is free
atom	divine_beneficent	the divine is beneficent
atom	man_beneficent	man is beneficent
atom	divine_magnanimous	the divine is magnanimous
atom	man_magnanimous	man is magnanimous
atom	imitator_of_god	man is an imitator of God
atom	act_consistently	man does and says everything consistently with being an imitator of God
